"Internal Error ()\nciteturn55view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.conseil-constitutionnel.fr/en/constitution-of-4-october-1958\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.conseil-constitutionnel.fr/en/constitution-of-4-october-1958: (429) Too Many Requests--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArticle II | U.S. Constitution | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute (https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/articleii)\nciteturn55view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/articleii\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 48\nL0: Please help us improve our site!\nL1: \nL2: 【0†No thank you】\nL3: \nL4: # Article II\nL5: \nL6: ## Primary tabs\nL7: ## Section 1.\nL8: \nL9: The executive power shall be vested in a President of the United States of America. He shall hold his office during the term of four years, and, together with the Vice President, chosen for the same term, be elected, as follows:\nL10: Each state shall appoint, in such manner as the Legislature thereof may direct, a number of electors, equal to the whole number of Senators and Representatives to which the State may be entitled in the Congress: but no Senator or Representative, or person holding an office of trust or profit under the United States, shall be appointed an elector.\nL11: 【1†The electors shall meet in their respective states, and vote by ballot for two persons, of whom one at least shall not be an inhabitant of the same state with themselves. And they shall make a list of all the persons voted for, and of the number of votes for each; which list they shall sign and certify, and transmit sealed to the seat of the government of the United States, directed to the President of the Senate.\nL12: The President of the Senate shall, in the presence of the Senate and House of Representatives, open all the certificates, and the votes shall then be counted.\nL13: The person having the greatest number of votes shall be the President, if such number be a majority of the whole number of electors appointed; and if there be more than one who have such majority, and have an equal number of votes, then the House of Representatives shall immediately choose by ballot one of them for President; and if no person have a majority, then from the five highest on the list the said House shall in like manner choose the President.\nL14: But in choosing the President, the votes shall be taken by States, the representation from each state having one vote; A quorum for this purpose shall consist of a member or members from two thirds of the states, and a majority of all the states shall be necessary to a choice. In every case, after the choice of the President, the person having the greatest number of votes of the electors shall be the Vice President.\nL15: But if there should remain two or more who have equal votes, the Senate shall choose from them by ballot the Vice President.】\nL16: The Congress may determine the time of choosing the electors, and the day on which they shall give their votes; which day shall be the same throughout the United States.\nL17: No person except a natural born citizen, or a citizen of the United States, at the time of the adoption of this Constitution, shall be eligible to the office of President; neither shall any person be eligible to that office who shall not have attained to the age of thirty five years, and been fourteen Years a resident within the United States.\nL18: 【2†In case of the removal of the President from office, or of his death, resignation, or inability to discharge the powers and duties of the said office, the same shall devolve on the Vice President, and the Congress may by law provide for the case of removal, death, resignation or inability, both of the President and Vice President, declaring what officer shall then act as President, and such officer shall act accordingly, until the disability be removed, or a President shall be elected.】\nL19: The President shall, at stated times, receive for his services, a compensation, which shall neither be increased nor diminished during the period for which he shall have been elected, and he shall not receive within that period any other emolument from the United States, or any of them.\nL20: Before he enter on the execution of his office, he shall take the following oath or affirmation:--\"I do solemnly swear (or affirm) that I will faithfully execute the office of President of the United States, and will to the best of my ability, preserve, protect and defend the Constitution of the United States.\"\nL21: ## Section 2.\nL22: \nL23: The President shall be commander in chief of the Army and Navy of the United States, and of the militia of the several states, when called into the actual service of the United States; he may require the opinion, in writing, of the principal officer in each of the executive departments, upon any subject relating to the duties of their respective offices, and he shall have power to grant reprieves and pardons for offenses against the United States, except in cases of impeachment.\nL24: He shall have power, by and with the advice and consent of the Senate, to make treaties, provided two thirds of the Senators present concur; and he shall nominate, and by and with the advice and consent of the Senate, shall appoint ambassadors, other public ministers and consuls, judges of the Supreme Court, and all other officers of the United States, whose appointments are not herein otherwise provided for, and which shall be established by law: but the Congress may by law vest the appointment of such inferior officers, as they think proper, in the President alone, in the courts of law, or in the heads of departments.\nL25: The President shall have power to fill up all vacancies that may happen during the recess of the Senate, by granting commissions which shall expire at the end of their next session.\nL26: ## Section 3.\nL27: He shall from time to time give to the Congress information of the state of the union, and recommend to their consideration such measures as he shall judge necessary and expedient; he may, on extraordinary occasions, convene both Houses, or either of them, and in case of disagreement between them, with respect to the time of adjournment, he may adjourn them to such time as he shall think proper; he shall receive ambassadors and other public ministers; he shall take care that the laws be faithfully executed, and shall commission all the officers of the United States.\nL28: ## Section 4.\nL29: \nL30: The President, Vice President and all civil officers of the United States, shall be removed from office on impeachment for, and conviction of, treason, bribery, or other high crimes and misdemeanors.\nL31: \nL32: 【3†‹ Article I】 【4†up】 【5†Article III ›】\nL33: ## U.S. Constitution Toolbox\nL34: \nL35:   * 【6†Explanation of the Constitution】 - from the Congressional Research Service\nL36: \nL37: * * *\nL38: \nL39: * * *\nL40: \nL41:   * 【7†Accessibility】\nL42:   * 【8†About LII】\nL43:   * 【9†Contact us】\nL44:   * 【10†Advertise here】\nL45:   * 【11†Help】\nL46:   * 【12†Terms of use】\nL47:   * 【13†Privacy】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe referendum (https://www.ch.ch/en/votes-and-elections/referendum/)\nciteturn55view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ch.ch/en/votes-and-elections/referendum/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 179\nL0: ## What would you like to know?\nL1: \nL2: How much does the motorway vignette cost?\nL3: \nL4: The AI (artificial intelligence) search function, currently available on a trial basis, only uses information from the ch.ch website. It may generate an incomplete or inaccurate response. The Federal Chancellery cannot guarantee the accuracy of the responses provided. 【0†Find out more.】\nL5: \nL6: I prefer 【1†the search without AI.】\nL7: \nL8: 【2†Homepage】【3†Main navigation】【4†language selection】\nL9: \nL10: [Select]\nL11: \nL12: Easy answers about life in Switzerland\nL13: 【5†All themes】\nL14: # The referendum\nL15: \nL16: The referendum is a cornerstone of direct democracy. It gives the voting public the right to vote on a new law. Find out here when and how a referendum takes place.\nL17: ## Note\nL18: \nL19: 【6†Image†livingdocs-ch-ch-prod.imgix.net】\nL20: \nL21: The optional referendum was introduced in 1874. Since then, around 200 optional referendums have been held, and about 40 per cent of the laws proposed have been rejected by the voters.\nL22: \nL23: [Button: Open all]\nL24: \nL25: [Input]The optional referendum\nL26: \nL27: Laws are made by Parliament. Most laws come into force without a referendum. But sometimes a vote is held on a new or amended law. This type of vote is called a referendum.\nL28: Most laws are subject to an optional referendum. This type of vote is only held when someone is against the new or amended law and so launches a referendum.\nL29: \nL30: Optional referendums can be held at national (federal), 【7†cantonal and communal level】.\nL31: At the federal level, the process is as follows: a referendum committee is usually set up and if it manages to collect 50,000 valid signatures within 100 days of the publication of the new legislation, a referendum is held. The legislation only comes into force if it is accepted by a 【8†majority of the voters】.\nL32: Parliament also decides on enactments that are not laws, for example decisions on international treaties between Switzerland and other countries. It is also possible to launch an optional referendum against these.\nL33: \nL34: [Input]The mandatory referendum\nL35: Most laws and other enactments passed by Parliament come into force without a popular vote being held. But in some cases, a referendum must be held. So unlike in the case of an 【9†optional referendum】, signatures do not have to be collected; the referendum is mandatory.\nL36: \nL37: This is always the case when Parliament wishes to amend the Constitution. Amendments to the Constitution require a majority vote of both the 【10†electorate and the cantons】.\nL38: Mandatory referendums are also held 【7†in the cantons and communes】.\nL39: \nL40: How is a referendum launched?\nL41: \nL42: 1 / 0\nL43: ## Who can launch a referendum?\nL44: \nL45: Any member of the electorate who is opposed to a new or amended law passed by the Swiss parliament can launch an optional referendum – even if they live abroad.\nL46: \nL47: Individuals can join forces with other people to form a referendum committee. This makes things easier, but is not absolutely necessary.\nL48: What is more, several groups can initiate a referendum against the same new or amended law – even though they might be from different political camps. The signatures collected are counted.\nL49: ## What's the first step?\nL50: \nL51: The Swiss Federal Chancellery can provide information on the steps to be taken to launch a referendum, including legal aspects.\nL52: \nL53: Swiss Federal Chancellery\nL54: Political Rights Section\nL55: Federal Palace West Wing\nL56: 3003 Bern\nL57: Tel. +41 58 462 48 02\nL58: spr@bk.admin.ch\nL59: \nL60: For referendums at cantonal or communal level, contact the 【7†relevant office at your canton or commune】.\nL61: ## Creating signature lists\nL62: \nL63: First of all, signature lists need to be created. This should be done early on, if possible while Parliament is still debating the new law or amendment so that signatures can be collected as soon as the law is published in the Federal Gazette.\nL64: The signature lists must state the exact title of the contested law, the date it was adopted by the Federal Assembly and certain other details. On request, the Federal Chancellery can provide a signature list template, like the one pictured above.\nL65: \nL66: Signature lists can also be checked by the Federal Chancellery.\nL67: ## Collecting signatures\nL68: \nL69: 【6†Image†livingdocs-ch-ch-prod.imgix.net】\nL70: \nL71: Signatures can only be collected once the contested law has been published in the Federal Gazette. From this point on, the authors of the referendum have 100 days to collect the necessary 50,000 signatures, have them certified and submit them to the Federal Chancellery.\nL72: \nL73: Anyone with the 【11†right to vote in Switzerland】 may sign.\nL74: They must enter their surname and first name, date of birth and address legibly and by hand in the list and sign it. A further requirement is that any given signature list may contain only the signatures of persons from the same commune – because the communes are the first to check the signatures.\nL75: \nL76: Usually not all signatures are found to be valid. It is therefore recommended to collect more than 50,000 signatures.\nL77: ## Signature verification\nL78: \nL79: All signatures collected must be verified by the communes before the collection deadline. This takes time, so the signature lists should be submitted to the communes on an ongoing basis.\nL80: \nL81: The communes check that the persons who have put their signature to the referendum are entered in the electoral roll and whether a name appears more than once.\nL82: ## Submitting the referendum\nL83: \nL84: All verified signatures must be submitted to the Federal Chancellery within 100 days. More information on this can be found in the 【12†factsheet on submitting referendums†www.bk.admin.ch】.\nL85: \nL86: The Federal Chancellery then checks that all the signatures are valid, in particular whether they comply with the legal requirements.\nL87: \nL88: If at least 50,000 signatures are valid, then the referendum request is successful.\nL89: ## Voting\nL90: \nL91: If the optional referendum request is successful, the electorate votes on the contested law. The Federal Council decides when the vote takes place. It must announce the date at least four months in advance.\nL92: \nL93: [Button: Open all]\nL94: \nL95: [Input]Who can add their signature to an optional referendum?\nL96: \nL97: All persons with the 【13†right to vote in Switzerland】 can sign an optional referendum.\nL98: 【14†Swiss citizens living abroad who are entitled to vote】 can also sign an optional referendum. To do so, they must be registered with the Swiss representation responsible for them.\nL99: \nL100: [Input]Referendums for which signatures are currently being collected\nL101: \nL102: The Federal Chancellery maintains a 【15†list of bills†www.bk.admin.ch】 against which a referendum can still be launched and for which signatures can be collected.\nL103: Signature lists can be downloaded directly (PDF) and printed out, usually from the website of the referendum committee. Completed lists can be sent by post to the address found on them.\nL104: \nL105: You can find a 【16†list of all the optional referendums submitted since 1874†www.bk.admin.ch】 on the Federal Chancellery website.\nL106: \nL107: [Input]Cantonal and communal referendums\nL108: Referendum are not only held against federal laws. Voters in a canton or a commune may launch an optional referendum if they are against a decision of the local parliament. The mandatory referendum also exists at these levels.\nL109: \nL110: Take a look at the website of your canton or commune if you want to know what can be contested and what requirements apply. The local authorities can answer any questions you may have about the procedure.\nL111: \nL112:   * 【17†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.ag.ch】\nL113:   * 【18†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.ai.ch】\nL114: \nL115:   * 【19†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.ar.ch】\nL116: \nL117:   * 【20†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.sta.be.ch】\nL118: \nL119:   * 【21†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.baselland.ch】\nL120: \nL121:   * 【22†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.staatskanzlei.bs.ch】\nL122: \nL123:   * 【23†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.fr.ch】\nL124: \nL125:   * 【24†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.ge.ch】\nL126:   * 【25†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.gl.ch】\nL127: \nL128:   * 【26†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.gr.ch】\nL129: \nL130:   * 【27†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.jura.ch】\nL131: \nL132:   * 【28†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†gemeinden.lu.ch】\nL133: \nL134:   * 【29†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.ne.ch】\nL135: \nL136:   * 【30†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.nw.ch】\nL137: \nL138:   * 【31†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.ow.ch】\nL139:   * 【32†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.sg.ch】\nL140: \nL141:   * 【33†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†sh.ch】\nL142: \nL143:   * 【34†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†so.ch】\nL144: \nL145:   * 【35†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.sz.ch】\nL146: \nL147:   * 【36†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†rechtsdienst.tg.ch】\nL148: \nL149:   * 【37†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www4.ti.ch】\nL150: \nL151:   * 【38†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.ur.ch】\nL152:   * 【39†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.vd.ch】\nL153: \nL154:   * 【40†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.vs.ch】\nL155: \nL156:   * 【41†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†zg.ch】\nL157: \nL158:   * 【42†How to launch a cantonal or communal referendum†www.zh.ch】\nL159: \nL160: [Input]Further information\nL161: \nL162:   * 【43†Further information on referendums†www.bk.admin.ch】 in Switzerland can be found on the Federal Chancellery website.\nL163:   * You can find out how 【44†voting rights for Swiss citizens】 are regulated on the page on voting rights.\nL164: \nL165:   * You can also read about 【45†voting rights for Swiss living abroad】.\nL166: \nL167:   * All 【15†bills with an ongoing referendum deadline†www.bk.admin.ch】 can be viewed on the Federal Chancellery website.\nL168: \nL169:   * The Federal Chancellery website lists 【46†all the optional referendums†www.bk.admin.ch】 that have been submitted since 1874.\nL170:   * The 【47†Political Rights Section†www.bk.admin.ch】 can answer any further questions you may have about referendums in Switzerland.\nL171: \nL172: 【5†All themes】\nL173: \nL174: A service of the Confederation, cantons and communes\nL175: \nL176: 【48†About ch.ch】【0†Terms and conditions】\nL177: \nL178: 【49†VoteInfo】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAbout participatory democracy - Participatory democracy (https://www.coe.int/en/web/participatory-democracy/about-participatory-democracy)\nciteturn55view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.coe.int/en/web/participatory-democracy/about-participatory-democracy\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 34\nL0: # Navigation\nL1: \nL2:   * 【0†Skip to Content】\nL3: \nL4: Participatory democracy\nL5: \nL6:   1. 【1†Home】\nL7:   2. 【2†Newsroom】\nL8:   3. 【3†Calendar】\nL9:   4. 【4†Projects】\nL10:   5. 【5†Documents】\nL11:   6. 【6†Covid-19 Response】\nL12:   7. 【7†Contact】\nL13: \nL14: # About participatory democracy\nL15: ## What is participatory democracy and why is it important?\nL16: \nL17: \nL18: \nL19: Citizens’ interests and needs should be the focus of every political decision-making process at all different governance levels. It is the heart of democracy that citizens’ concerns are the basis for change. Participatory processes allow exactly for that– they give citizens the possibility to take part in decision-making and to give impulse for change.\nL20: There are 【8†different levels of participatory democracy practices†rm.coe.int】 (information, consultation, dialogue and partnership and many different models to organise participation. Be it a user-friendly interactive municipality homepage, a consultation platform, a Participatory Budgeting initiative or a Citizens Assembly, the aim is always to engage citizens or civil society actors in local, regional, or national decision-making processes on issues that affect their daily life.\nL21: The Division of Elections and Participatory Democracy supports public authorities and civil society stakeholders, to establish sustainable mechanisms for civil participation. It also provides innovative tools (“【9†Toolkits】”), to implement participatory processes and provides capacity building and trainings for practitioners as well as legal support to create the necessary regulatory framework.\nL22: Through round table events, expert discussions and public campaigns, it raises local authorities’ and civil society organisations’ awareness builds knowledge regarding International and European participatory standards and mechanisms.\nL23: Currently, the Division implements 【10†co-operation projects to strengthen participatory democracy】 in Georgia and in Ukraine. Projects in Bosnia and Herzegovina, the Republic of Moldova and Morocco are in the planning.\nL24: The Division works closely with the 【11†Conference of International Non-Governmental Organisations】 (CINGO), the 【12†Congress of Local and Regional Authorities】 and other 【13†Council of Europe entities】, who focus on the protection of Human Rights defenders, on the development of Civil Society Organisations and on the political participation of specific sectors for society.\nL25: \nL26: 【14†Image】\nL27: \nL28: Useful links\nL29: ####\nL30: \nL31: #### 【15†Electoral assistance】\nL32: \nL33: #### 【16†Image†static.coe.int】 【17†Contact us】\n"
